    <dcterms:title>Euthyphro</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:description>Wikipedia page about this book: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euthyphro</dcterms:description>
    <pgterms:marc508>Produced by Sue Asscher, and David Widger</pgterms:marc508>
    <pgterms:marc520>"Euthyphro" by Plato is a philosophical dialogue written around 399 BC. Set outside an Athenian court, it follows Socrates as he awaits trial for impiety. He encounters Euthyphro, a confident prophet prosecuting his own father for murder. Socrates asks Euthyphro to define piety, sparking a rigorous examination that produces increasingly refined definitions—yet none prove satisfactory. Their discussion culminates in a famous dilemma about the gods' relationship to goodness, launching a debate that continues millennia later. (This is an automatically generated summary.)</pgterms:marc520>
    <pgterms:marc908>Reading ease score: 76.0 (7th grade). Fairly easy to read.</pgterms:marc908>
